<GuideLayout slug="best-fortnite-landing-spots">
  <p>
    Picking where to drop is half of every Fortnite match — and most guides stop at "go where
    the loot is." The other half is <strong>getting there first</strong>, which is pure timing
    and bus geometry. Here's how to think about both.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing the spot: three trade-offs</h2>
  <h3>Loot density vs. contest risk</h3>
  <p>
    Named POIs have the best loot and the most enemies. Landing a named spot means you either
    win the early fight or die with nothing. If you're warming up or playing for placement,
    a quieter landmark on the POI's edge gets you geared without the coin-flip.
  </p>
  <h3>Rotation, not just loot</h3>
  <p>
    A great spot you can't leave is a trap. Look at where the first circles tend to pull and
    whether you have a clean rotation — high ground, a vehicle, or a low-traffic path. Landing
    on the wrong side of the map costs you the mid-game.
  </p>
  <h3>Land high, drop down</h3>
  <p>
    Within a POI, the highest building or hill is the spot to grab first. Height = first weapon,
    first shots, and the option to disengage. Roll-land to keep full HP.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting there first: the part nobody calculates</h2>
  <p>
    Whoever lands first wins the contest before it starts. In the current glider-cut meta,
    that's decided by exactly where you jump and where you cut the glider — not by holding
    forward and hoping. The physics are fixed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The bus moves at <strong>75 m/s</strong> at <strong>832 m</strong> altitude.</li>
    <li>Free-fall is fast; the glider auto-deploys at <strong>100 m</strong> above ground.</li>
    <li>The fastest line is to <strong>cut the glider</strong>, nosedive, and only glide at the end — the cut point is somewhere between the bus and your target, never right on top of either.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Eyeballing that cut point is guesswork, and being half a second late loses the fight. This
    is the entire reason we built <strong>Dropmaxxer</strong>: pick any POI and it gives you the
    fastest cut-and-free-fall line and the safe glide line, both ETAs, and the exact spot to
    cut — from cited physics, not vibes.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Pick a spot you can rotate out of, land the high ground inside it, and cut your glider at
    the right point so you're the one holding a gun when the fight starts.
  </blockquote>
</GuideLayout>
